# Typed Pharmacological Evidence and Connectome-Dependence Testing: A Computational Method for Drug Simulation on Cuts of a Synapse-Resolution *Drosophila* Connectome

**Research article, submitted draft for the International Journal of Research in Computing (IJRC)**

Software: https://github.com/pinkysworld/FlyLab
Version {{flylab_version}}, commit `{{git_sha}}`, compound library SHA-256 `{{library_sha256}}`, map `{{map_id}}`.

> Every numeric span in this document is generated by `scripts/reproduce_paper.py` from `papers/results.json`; the prose is written around the numbers, not the other way round. No number in this paper was measured in a living animal.

## Abstract

A drug prediction made on a wiring diagram has no way to say which of its inputs it depends on. We present FlyLab, a method and an implementation that couples published pharmacological evidence to compound- and concentration-specific perturbation of named cells in two hops-limited cuts of the public MaleCNS v1.0 adult *Drosophila* central nervous system. The coupling rests on a **type system for pharmacological evidence**, in which the parameter a source measured and its graded distance from the modelled compound-receptor-species triple jointly decide which transformation is permitted, under a soundness invariant checked exhaustively over the library: unsupported receptors return N/A rather than a plausible small number, and {{ev_rows_not_modelled}} of {{ev_rows}} rows are refused. Three tests sit on top of it: **connectome-dependence analysis**, which places each prediction on an information ladder of degraded graphs and reports, against a prespecified equivalence margin and with multiplicity controlled, the weakest graph model the test cannot distinguish from the real cut; an **ablation ladder**; and a **conclusion-stability matrix** re-deriving every qualitative claim under {{stab_family_size}} admissible engagement-to-gain transformations. Our principal empirical result is a negative one about the method itself. On the 1-hop `named` cut — mean degree {{named_mean_degree}}, {{named_share_onto_seeds}} of edges terminating on four seed cells — {{dep_land_composition_dominated}} of {{dep_land_cells}} compound-concentration cells cannot be distinguished from any structure-preserving degradation of the graph and {{dep_land_topology_dependent}} need the wiring; repeated unchanged on the denser `taste_motor` cut (mean degree {{taste_motor_mean_degree}}), the same instrument returns {{dep_land_taste_composition_dominated}} composition-dominated of {{dep_land_taste_cells}} and {{dep_land_taste_topology_dependent}} topology-dependent. The verdict is a property of the substrate, and a validated instrument that reverses between two cuts of one connectome is a more cautionary and more transferable result than the majority it used to report. The central limitation is unchanged: FlyLab evaluates the behaviour of a model, and its gain rules are asserted rather than measured.

**Index terms:** computational method, connectomics, *Drosophila melanogaster*, in-silico pharmacology, evidence typing, null models, equivalence testing, multiple comparisons, specification robustness, reproducible research, 3Rs.

## 1. Introduction

A public synapse-resolution connectome invites a question it cannot answer on its own: *at the concentration at which a compound engages its insect target and not its vertebrate counterpart, what happens to a named cell?* Answering it means joining two literatures with incompatible units: potency parameters measured in heterologous preparations, often in another species and frequently absent altogether, and a netlist with a predicted transmitter per cell and no ligand, no concentration and no receptor subtype.

Joining them is not, by itself, a contribution. Executable circuits built from fly connectome data already exist as a platform [68]; leaky integrate-and-fire models over the whole adult brain recover sensorimotor controls without fitting [7]; and whole-brain models are routinely perturbed through receptor-density maps [69], [70]. What is missing is a way to answer, for each prediction, *which of its inputs it depends on*.

We organise the work around four research questions.

* **RQ1.** Can heterogeneous published pharmacological evidence be turned into computational perturbations *without* erasing its provenance or its parameter semantics?
* **RQ2.** Which pharmacological effects require the specific MaleCNS topology, and which are reproduced by coarse network statistics?
* **RQ3.** Which conclusions survive alternative admissible specifications, and which assumptions dominate the model's uncertainty?
* **RQ4.** Can the entire analysis be reproduced identically, natively and in a browser, from auditable artifacts?

The answers are: yes, by grading the evidence and refusing invalid transformations; **it depends on the cut**, and that dependence is the result; not all of them, and an undocumented normalisation inside the engine carries part of RQ2's answer; and yes. A previous version answered RQ2 with "a minority of predictions, and they are a chemically coherent minority", measured on one cut.

### 1.1 Related work and what is new here

**Virtual animals without neurons.** Teaching simulators [1]–[4] implement organ-level dose–response and contain no named cell.

**Connectome runtimes without pharmacology.** FlyWire [5] and MaleCNS v1.0 [6] publish netlists with per-cell transmitter predictions [10], [12]; Shiu *et al.* [7] show a literature-parameterised LIF model over such a netlist reproduces sugar-driven motor output and its bitter veto, and downstream ports repeat the exercise with varying rigour [11]. FlyBrainLab [68] builds executable circuits directly from fly brain data, but a compound, if present, enters as a hand-set multiplier with no receptor and no concentration behind it.

**Receptor-informed whole-brain models.** On human structural connectomes, intervention is simulated by scaling regional synaptic responses with PET receptor-density maps [69], [70]. This is the closest published relative, and its most relevant result is a negative one: in Mindlin *et al.* [69] a simulated intervention's improvement correlated with the *mean density of the activated receptors across the brain* rather than with where those receptors sit. Methodologically, §2.4 is a multiverse analysis [74], [75] applied to a simulation, and §2.6 uses Saltelli cross-sampling [71] with Jansen estimators [72] validated on Ishigami [73] and ranked by value of information [76].

**The evidence base and the methodological neighbours.** Insect-versus-vertebrate receptor pharmacology supplies the library — nicotinic [8], [9], [26], [35]–[37], GABA-gated chloride [16], [27], [29], [31] with the β3-homopentamer caveat [32], glutamate-gated chloride [28], [33], [38], sodium channels with differential *kdr* sensitivity [23], [67], resistance alleles [13]–[25], expression atlases [39]–[44] and mixture null models [45]–[51] — and `data/literature/README.md` records ten places where those sources contradict the shipped library, including fipronil at `vertebrate_GABA_A` about nine-fold off its own citation, without editing the library to match.

**Position.** Table I states what is new narrowly. It is the combination of typed pharmacological evidence, compound- and concentration-specific perturbation of *named, synapse-resolution* circuits, an insect/vertebrate comparison at one dose, and — the transferable part — routine, validated, per-prediction testing of whether a prediction needs the connectome at all.

**Table I. What each system provides.**

| Capability | Teaching sims [1]–[4] | Connectome LIF runtimes [7], [11] | FlyBrainLab [68] | Receptor-map whole-brain models [69], [70] | FlyLab |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Named cells at synapse resolution | no | yes | yes | no (regions) | yes |
| Compound **and free concentration** as the input | yes (organ level) | no | no | partly (receptor-map scaling) | yes |
| Typed evidence with per-row provenance and distance | no | no | no | no | yes |
| Two species scored at the same dose | no | no | no | no | yes |
| Dependence of each prediction on the wiring, tested | no | rarely | no | partly [69] | yes, routinely |
| The dependence test itself validated against ground truth | n/a | no | no | no | yes (§3.2, F16) |
| Specification robustness of the stated conclusions | no | no | no | no | yes |
| Whole analysis re-runs in a browser | some | no | no | no | endpoint parity verified |

We claim no new reconstruction, no fitted *in vivo* potency, no replacement for regulatory toxicology and no live-animal result. The `live_lab` field of every notebook this pipeline produced is `null`.

## 2. Methods

### 2.1 Typed pharmacological evidence (RQ1)

The v0.5 model, like most of this literature, applied a Hill function to whatever number a source reported and called the result *receptor occupancy*. That is wrong twice over: `C^n/(EC50^n + C^n)` on a functional potency is a normalised response and not a fraction of binding sites occupied, and a row with no sourced number must not yield a small response at all.

FlyLab types the evidence instead. Each row records the **parameter type** its source measured (`Kd`, `Ki`, `EC50`, `IC50`, `Kb`, `relative_potency`, `class_order`, `unknown`) and an ordered **evidence distance** between that source and this compound at this receptor in this species: {{ev_distance_labels}}. The permitted **engagement model** is a function of that pair (Table T20): a binding constant yields physical fractional occupancy only at E0 and a labelled `binding_engagement_proxy` beyond it, a functional potency yields `functional_engagement` at E0 and `functional_engagement_proxy` beyond, and anything at E4 is `not_modelled` and returns N/A. We say *engagement* where the parameter is functional, *proxy* where it was transferred, and reserve *occupancy* for the rows that earn it.

Two properties make this a type system rather than a lookup table. The refusal is enforced, not conventional: requesting an engagement from an unsupported row raises `EvidenceTypeError`. And we state and check a **soundness invariant** over the whole pipeline — *no numeric engagement reaching a circuit readout may originate from a row whose (parameter type, distance) pair does not admit one* — exhaustively, over every row and every entry point, with a companion check that dropping the inadmissible rows changes no gain; mutating the rule table makes the test fail with the right message. We claim the invariant and its check as the transferable computing contribution.

### 2.2 The circuit substrate, and what the engine does to it

Two committed cuts of MaleCNS v1.0 are used, streamed out of the ~1.1 GB public weight matrix on a CI runner and each under about 1 MB of JSON: **`named`**, a 1-hop neighbourhood of `MN9` and `DNp01` ({{named_nodes}} nodes, {{named_edges}} edges), and **`taste_motor`**, the same seeds plus the labellar gustatory types and an induced closure ({{taste_nodes}} nodes, {{taste_edges}} edges).

**The two cuts are structurally very different, and the difference is load-bearing (Table T19).** The `named` cut has mean degree {{named_mean_degree}}: {{named_share_onto_seeds}} of its edges terminate on the {{named_n_seed_nodes}} seed cells, only {{named_nodes_with_input}} of {{named_nodes}} nodes receive any input, and only {{named_share_recurrent_edges}} of edges leave a node that itself receives input. The `taste_motor` cut has mean degree {{taste_motor_mean_degree}}, {{taste_motor_share_onto_seeds}} of edges onto seeds and {{taste_motor_share_recurrent_edges}} of edges leaving a node with input. Transmitter censuses differ too: the `named` cut carries {{named_cells_acetylcholine}} cholinergic, {{named_cells_gaba}} GABAergic, {{named_cells_glutamate}} glutamatergic and **{{named_cells_octopamine}}** octopaminergic cells, with {{named_share_unclear_cells}} of cells carrying no consensus prediction and therefore a presynaptic sign of zero. A compound whose only insect mechanism acts through octopamine cannot move either cut, which is a fact about the substrate rather than the compound.

Two runtimes consume a gain patch: a deterministic clipped leaky rate iteration (~50 ms per evaluation of `named`,) and a current-based LIF network [7] whose synaptic scale and background drive are tuned constants, not fits (§S3).

**The rate engine row-normalises its weight matrix, and previous versions of this paper did not say so.** Each row of the signed matrix is divided by `max(Σ|W|, 1)` before any gain is applied, and gains then act as a presynaptic column scale, so every cell's recurrent input is a *composition-weighted average of its presynaptic gains*. That builds part of "composition-dominated" into the readout RQ2 tests, so it is documented here, in T2 and in §S3, and swept in §3.3 against `none` and a degree-corrected denominator. The transmitter signs the engine iterates are published as Table T21; {{sign_asserted}} of the seven ({{sign_asserted_transmitters}}) are asserted magnitudes with no measurement behind them, varied by no member of the specification family and not bounded by the label-permuting null, which permutes assignments and leaves the table untouched.

### 2.3 Connectome-dependence analysis (RQ2)

The instrument we consider most transferable asks, for one `compound × concentration × circuit × readout`, how much of the real connectome a prediction needs. The same drug contrast (treated minus vehicle, both arms on the *same* graph) is measured on progressively degraded copies of the cut, which form an **information ladder**:

| Graph model | What it still knows |
|---|---|
| `erdos_renyi` | node and edge counts, the weight histogram, the transmitter census |
| `rewire_degree_preserving` | + every node's in-degree, out-degree and transmitter |
| `weight_permute` | + the real edge list (weights reshuffled) |
| `sign_permute_weight_matched` | + the real weights on the real edges, and each transmitter's share of total outgoing weight |
| the MaleCNS cut | everything |

The rank-3 rung changed in this revision. Because a gain patch acts *through* transmitter identity, a null that moves the weighted balance moves the drug's target set and the sign matrix at once, so the plain label permutation is relabelled a **joint target-set-and-sign null** and replaced by a constrained shuffle holding every tracked transmitter's share of outgoing weight within {{bal_matched_max_deviation}} of the real graph's. Permuting the node transmitter-label array preserves the transmitter histogram *by count* but not *by weight*: out-strength is heterogeneous, so on the `named` cut the cholinergic share of total outgoing synaptic weight is {{bal_real_ach_share}} on the real graph against {{bal_plain_ach_mean}} ± {{bal_plain_ach_sd}} across {{bal_n}} permutations, putting the real graph at the {{bal_plain_ach_percentile}}th percentile of its own null (T23).

**Non-rejection is not equivalence, and the software no longer lets the prose say it is.** Each mode receives one of three verdicts against a margin prespecified as {{dep_named_imidacloprid_delta_frac}} of the vehicle readout ({{dep_named_imidacloprid_delta}} Hz on `named`): `distinguishable` when the empirical two-sided permutation probability `(k+1)/(n+1)` is at or below α; `equivalent_within_tolerance` when the test does not reject *and* the gap from the null median is below the margin; `indeterminate` otherwise. From the verdicts follow a **class** and a **necessary information level** — the weakest graph model the test could not distinguish from the real cut, never a claim that it reproduces the effect. Where a weaker model survives while a richer one is rejected the ladder is non-monotone, which the software reports and §3.2 counts.

Three further conventions are prespecified and §S2 states them in full: a *relative* effect floor of 1 % of the vehicle readout; a separation of **confirmatory** tests (the two profiles at *n* ≥ 1000, uncorrected) from **exploratory** ones (a landscape, Benjamini-Hochberg corrected across its structural tests with raw and adjusted counts both reported); and a check of how many tests must sit at the resolution floor before anything can be rejected, so a corrected count of zero is never mistaken for a finding. The instrument is also evaluated against ground truth: a recurrent cholinergic cycle of known strength is planted in a synthetic cut of the same size, density and composition as `named`, so that collapsing `g_ach` removes an amplification existing only while the cycle is intact, with strength 0 as the negative control (§3.2, F16).

### 2.4 The ablation ladder and the specification family (RQ3)

*Which layer carries the information?* Four models predict the same cell with one layer denied: **A** receptor engagement alone; **B** mechanism gains on the graph's transmitter composition only, no edges; **C** the real cut and runtime with one generic multiplier; **D** the full model. They carry different units, so they are compared by how well each reproduces the full model's *ordering* of the library, scored on the **signed** Spearman ρ because a perfect inversion reproduces nothing.

Level C's multiplier changed in this revision. It was `g_all = max(0.05, 1 − θ_max)` on every transmitter alike, which can only depress, so no compound could come out positive and the level could not order a library containing RDL and GluCl blockers. The magnitude is unchanged but the **sign** now comes from the mechanism table — one bit per compound, and nothing about which transmitter or by how much — and the old rule is retained as a *floor*. Levels B and D are not independent models, both being functions of the same gain vector, so §3.3 reports a **matched reference distribution** over pharmacology-free pseudo-compounds plus the correlation under a permutation of the compound labels.

*Which conclusions are properties of the data rather than of a modelling choice?* The mapping from engagement to synaptic gain is the least defensible part of the model: the shipped rule for an insect nicotinic agonist, `g_ach = max(0.05, 1 + 0.4θ − 1.6θ²)`, encodes excitation followed by desensitisation and depolarisation block — a textbook picture, but an asserted functional form. We prespecify {{stab_family_size}} admissible specifications (five shapes at five coefficient scales, §S1.1) and re-derive every principal conclusion under every member; undecidable cells count against the conclusion and are reported separately, and the fraction is a fraction of *that family*, not a posterior probability. The two topology conclusions are decided at {{stab_n_shuffles}} permutations per specification (resolution {{stab_shuffle_resolution}}) and corrected across the {{stab_n_structural_tests}} structural tests of the run. The two selectivity thresholds are likewise conventions, so the mechanism-level verdict is recomputed on the full grid of {{thr_circuit_fracs}} × {{thr_vert_limits}} over the {{thr_n_compounds}} compounds of the two mechanism classes.

### 2.5 Evaluation taxonomy

Agreement with the literature is worth little unless its kind is named, so we separate three. **Implementation consistency** — the same inputs give the same outputs across transports, engines and runs (§3.7). **Literature concordance** — the model's *ordering* of compounds agrees with published orderings, scored per entry and never as a bare mean, because an entry ordering two compounds can only return ±1 whatever the model does; where the published ordering comes from a paper the library already cites for one of the compounds it orders, agreement is internal consistency, and the pipeline computes that overlap from DOIs and PMIDs rather than asserting it. **Independent, out-of-sample validation** — a comparison against data that entered neither the library nor the model. FlyLab has **none** at the circuit level, and says so; §2.3's ground-truth experiment validates the *instrument*, not the biology.

### 2.6 Global uncertainty and the value of information

A one-at-a-time tornado cannot separate a factor's own effect from its interactions, so uncertainty is attributed with a variance-based (Sobol') analysis over every assumption the implementation exposes — potency, Hill coefficient, the engagement-to-gain transformation and its coefficient, the external drive, the cut's synapse-weight threshold, the transmitter-sign convention, the expression-weighting layer — plus a deliberately null factor, the spiking RNG seed, which the deterministic rate engine ignores. The design is Saltelli cross-sampling with Jansen estimators [71], [72], validated against the analytic Ishigami benchmark [73] in the same run.

Estimates are left unclipped, so a factor whose true index is zero can be estimated below zero. A first-order index cannot be negative, so the **largest magnitude among the negative estimates is a measured lower bound on the estimator's error**, and that — not the null factor's own draw — is the noise floor we report. The budget becomes a ranking by `VOI_j = max(0, S_j) · Var(Y)` [76] — variance of a model output under assumed input ranges, not an expected gain in accuracy about a living fly.

## 3. Results

Everything in this section is a property of the software, the typed library and two hops-limited MaleCNS cuts. All values are regenerated by `scripts/reproduce_paper.py` and stored keyed in `papers/results.json`.

### 3.1 RQ1: what the library actually rests on (Fig. F11, Tables T10, T20)

Of {{ev_rows}} rows over {{ev_compounds}} compounds and {{ev_receptor_keys}} receptor keys, {{ev_param_EC50}} carry an EC50, {{ev_param_IC50}} an IC50 and {{ev_param_Kd}} a binding constant; {{ev_rows_not_modelled}} carry no modellable evidence and return N/A. By evidence distance, {{ev_dist_E0}} rows are on-target, {{ev_dist_E1}} the right receptor in another species, {{ev_dist_E2}} a related or hybrid preparation, {{ev_dist_E3}} a class extrapolation and {{ev_dist_E4}} unsupported; the permitted transformations follow as {{ev_model_binding_occupancy}} `binding_occupancy`, {{ev_model_binding_engagement_proxy}} `binding_engagement_proxy`, {{ev_model_functional_engagement}} `functional_engagement`, {{ev_model_functional_engagement_proxy}} `functional_engagement_proxy` and {{ev_model_not_modelled}} `not_modelled`.

The single row entitled to a physical binding-occupancy model is **{{ev_binding_row_names}}** — a radioligand saturation Kd on *Drosophila* head membranes [77]. The library's previous Kd, {{ev_binding_proxy_row_names}}, was measured in an aphid and is now demoted to a cross-species proxy: the same number, under a name that says it was transferred. Without the *Drosophila* row the binding branch would have no instances at all.

At 1 µM, imidacloprid engages the insect nicotinic row at {{imi_insect_occ}} against {{imi_vert_occ}} at vertebrate α4β2 ({{imi_ec50_ratio}}-fold, {{window_log10_imidacloprid}} log₁₀ units of window), and fipronil engages insect RDL at {{fip_insect_occ}} against {{fip_vert_occ}} at native heteromeric vertebrate GABA-A ({{fip_ec50_ratio}}-fold) — the second contrast being the honest one, and the consequence of a library correction that removed a "vertebrate-safe" claim the v0.4 draft could have made in passing. Placeholder rows no longer contribute at all: diazepam at insect RDL reports "{{dia_insect_rdl_occ}}".

### 3.2 RQ2: which predictions need the connectome — and on which cut (Figs. F6, F12, F16; Tables T6, T11, T12, T12b, T22, T23)

**The instrument works.** On synthetic cuts with a planted recurrent cholinergic cycle the ladder recovered {{val_recovery_detected}} of {{val_recovery_positive}} planted effects at *n* = {{val_recovery_n}} with no false positive on the control, and over a power grid ({{val_power_replicates}} graphs per cell, permutation counts {{val_power_ns}}) the detection rate by planted strength is {{val_power_by_strength}}, full power at planted strength {{val_power_full_strengths}} and detection at or below one in four at {{val_power_weak_strengths}}. The empirical false-positive rate on the unplanted control is {{val_power_control_hits}} of {{val_power_control_runs}}, and averaged over strengths the detection rate by permutation count is {{val_power_by_n}}: **effect size dominates and permutation count buys little beyond a few hundred.**

**The confirmatory profiles.** At {{dep_n}} permutations per mode (resolution {{dep_p_floor}}), imidacloprid's effect on the `named` cut's mean rate is {{dep_named_imidacloprid_effect}} Hz and the cell is **{{dep_named_imidacloprid_class}}**. Against the prespecified margin of {{dep_named_imidacloprid_delta}} Hz the verdicts are: weight permutation {{dep_named_imidacloprid_verdict_weight_permute}} (*p* = {{dep_named_imidacloprid_p_weight_permute}}), degree-preserving rewiring {{dep_named_imidacloprid_verdict_rewire_degree_preserving}} (*p* = {{dep_named_imidacloprid_p_rewire_degree_preserving}}), the weight-matched transmitter null {{dep_named_imidacloprid_verdict_sign_permute_weight_matched}} (*p* = {{dep_named_imidacloprid_p_sign_permute_weight_matched}}), the joint label-permutation null {{dep_named_imidacloprid_verdict_sign_permute}} (*p* = {{dep_named_imidacloprid_p_sign_permute}}) and the Erdős–Rényi control {{dep_named_imidacloprid_verdict_erdos_renyi}} (*p* = {{dep_named_imidacloprid_p_erdos_renyi}}). Equivalence within tolerance is shown for {{dep_named_imidacloprid_equivalent_modes}} and *not* for {{dep_named_imidacloprid_indeterminate_modes}}; the necessary level is `{{dep_named_imidacloprid_level}}`, verdict {{dep_named_imidacloprid_level_verdict}}. Correcting the transmitter null does not move the probability but changes the equivalence claim: the gap from the null median falls from {{dep_named_imidacloprid_gap_sign_permute}} Hz to {{dep_named_imidacloprid_gap_sign_permute_weight_matched}} Hz. Fipronil is the contrast: {{dep_named_fipronil_effect}} Hz, **{{dep_named_fipronil_class}}**, weight permutation *p* = {{dep_named_fipronil_p_weight_permute}}, degree-preserving rewiring *p* = {{dep_named_fipronil_p_rewire_degree_preserving}}, necessary level `{{dep_named_fipronil_level}}`.

**The landscape reverses between the two cuts.** Over the library ({{dep_land_compounds}} compounds × {{dep_land_concs}} concentrations = {{dep_land_cells}} cells) at {{dep_n_landscape}} permutations, Benjamini-Hochberg across the {{dep_land_n_structural_tests}} structural tests and the relative effect floor applied, the `named` cut gives **{{dep_land_topology_dependent}} topology-dependent, {{dep_land_composition_dominated}} composition-dominated, {{dep_land_no_effect}} no-effect, {{dep_land_mixed}} mixed** (uncorrected {{dep_land_topology_dependent_raw}} / {{dep_land_composition_dominated_raw}} / {{dep_land_no_effect_raw}}; on the absolute floor alone {{dep_land_topology_dependent_absfloor}} / {{dep_land_composition_dominated_absfloor}} / {{dep_land_no_effect_absfloor}}, the {{dep_land_below_relative_floor}} cells the relative floor removes being named in T12). Repeated unchanged on `taste_motor` at {{dep_n_landscape_taste}} permutations, the same {{dep_land_taste_cells}} cells give **{{dep_land_taste_topology_dependent}} topology-dependent, {{dep_land_taste_composition_dominated}} composition-dominated, {{dep_land_taste_no_effect}} no-effect**. Every cell with an effect above the floor is topology-dependent, imidacloprid at 1 µM included: its degree-preserving-rewiring probability there is {{dep_land_taste_imidacloprid_p_rewire_degree_preserving}} (adjusted {{dep_land_taste_imidacloprid_q}}), class {{dep_land_taste_imidacloprid_class}}.

**The composition-dominated majority was a property of the substrate.** The `named` cut is an in-star whose degree-preserving rewire is close to the identity; the denser cut has wiring to destroy, and destroying it changes the answer for every compound the drug moves at all. We withdraw the generalisation that most of what a receptor-perturbation model predicts on a connectome is carried by composition rather than topology, and with it the claim that the topology-dependent set is exactly the chloride-channel blockers. What we can support is narrower and more useful: **the verdict this method returns is substrate-dependent, and the substrate must be reported with it.**

**Multiplicity, working.** As a prespecified confirmatory profile, fipronil at 1 µM stands comfortably (*p* = {{dep_named_fipronil_min_structural_p}} at *n* = {{dep_n}}). Inside the exploratory landscape the same cell's adjusted probability is {{dep_land_fipronil_q}} and it does **not** survive correction; its corrected class there is {{dep_land_fipronil_class}} against an uncorrected {{dep_land_fipronil_class_raw}}.

**Two things the class label does not say.** The ladder is **non-monotone** in {{dep_land_non_monotone}} of {{dep_land_cells}} cells on `named` and {{dep_land_taste_non_monotone}} of {{dep_land_taste_cells}} on `taste_motor`: a weaker graph model could not be told apart from the real cut while a richer one could, because they destroy different things, so the level means "the cheapest graph model this test cannot tell apart from the real one" and never "everything above it is indistinguishable too" (affected cells in T12). And `composition-dominated` says no structure-preserving degradation could be *told apart* from the real graph, never that those degradations reproduce the effect: across the `named` landscape's mode verdicts, {{dep_land_verdicts_equivalent_within_tolerance}} are equivalent within tolerance, {{dep_land_verdicts_indeterminate}} indeterminate and {{dep_land_verdicts_distinguishable}} distinguishable.

**Two negatives worth their own sentence.** At {{dep_n_taste}} permutations the fipronil bitter-veto ratio on the map path is not distinguishable from any shuffled control (max |z| = {{dep_taste_fipronil_max_abs_z}}), equivalent within tolerance for {{dep_taste_fipronil_equivalent_modes}} and indeterminate for {{dep_taste_fipronil_indeterminate_modes}}; imidacloprid has no veto ratio there at all, because at 1 µM it silences MN9 and the ratio is *undefined*, not zero. And the permutation-count sweep shows a *verdict* at α = 0.05 settling from *n* = {{dep_verdict_stable_from_n}}–{{dep_verdict_stable_to_n}} while a quotable mid-range probability needs {{dep_p_within_0p02_from_n}}–{{dep_p_within_0p02_worst_n}} draws to reach ±0.02 of its final value.

### 3.3 RQ2 continued: which layer carries the information (Fig. F13, Tables T13, T13b, T24, T25)

Receptor engagement alone orders the library **backwards** (signed ρ {{abl_rho_receptor_min}} to {{abl_rho_receptor_max}}) and is the worst level at {{abl_worst_level_by_conc}}; with the historical level-C rule it is {{abl_worst_level_by_conc_floor_rule}}, so the previous claim that the topology-only level is the worst of the four was true of one column of the table and false of the others.

**The ablation conclusion is withdrawn.** Level C's multiplier applied a depression-only factor to every transmitter, so every entry was at most zero and the level could not represent disinhibition. With a direction-aware rule — same generic magnitude, one bit of sign — its rank correlation with the full model at 1 µM rises from {{abl_rho_topology_floor_paper}} to {{abl_rho_topology_paper}}. The sentence "the connectome without the pharmacology is not a cheap substitute for the connectome with it" was a statement about a sign-broken baseline and is retracted.

**The composition-versus-full correlation carries no compound-level information.** It is {{abl_rho_composition_paper}} at 1 µM and {{abl_ref_no_floor}} after dropping the compounds on the 0.05 gain floor, so it is not a tie artefact. But levels B and D are both functions of the same gain vector, and the matched reference says what would have been unsurprising: pharmacology-free pseudo-compounds with the gain shape the shipped rules produce give a median of {{abl_ref_matched_median}} over {{abl_ref_n_draws}} draws (5th–95th percentile {{abl_ref_matched_p05}} to {{abl_ref_matched_p95}}), and permuting the compound labels leaves the observed value identically {{abl_ref_shuffled}}, as it must, because the permutation moves the (B, D) pairs as a block.

**And it is not robust to the engine.** Under the shipped row normalisation the correlation is {{abl_rho_composition_row_abs}}; removing the normalisation gives {{abl_rho_composition_none}}, but a degree-corrected denominator gives {{abl_rho_composition_degree}} at 1 µM and {{abl_rho_composition_degree_1e8}} at 10 nM. The verdict survives {{abl_composition_survives_normalisations}} and not the degree-corrected engine, so §3.3 is a property of *this engine's readout* at least as much as of receptor perturbation on connectomes. Levels B and D also disagree about the sign of glutamate, but reconciling them moves the correlation by at most {{abl_glutamate_max_delta_rho}}, so that defect is not what produces the agreement.

### 3.4 RQ3: which conclusions survive (Fig. F14, Tables T14, T14b, T15)

**The previous 25/25 topology result was vacuous, and we say so plainly.** Two things were wrong at once. The matrix was computed at 6 shuffles per specification, where the smallest attainable permutation probability is 1/7 = 0.143, so `p ≤ 0.05` was arithmetically impossible and the predicate fell back on a Gaussian criterion. And the specification context rebound the gain function on the assay modules but not on the engine the permutation path resolves, so **all {{stab_family_size}} specifications fed default gains to their nulls and produced identical effects**. The earlier evidence that the RQ2 results were specification-independent was empty, and that sentence licensed the old §3.2 headline.

Recomputed at {{stab_n_shuffles}} shuffles per specification (resolution {{stab_shuffle_resolution}}), with the specification pushed into the null engine and Benjamini-Hochberg applied across the {{stab_n_structural_tests}} structural tests, both topology conclusions still hold {{stab_C3_retained}}/{{stab_family_size}} and {{stab_C4_retained}}/{{stab_family_size}}. The nuance the old matrix could not show is that a retention rate for a conclusion shaped as a *failure to reject* is only as strong as the equivalence behind it: of C3's {{stab_C3_retained}}, {{stab_C3_equivalent}} reach equivalence within the prespecified margin and {{stab_C3_indeterminate}} are merely indeterminate. Under the {{stab_family_size}} specifications:

* "A nicotinic agonist suppresses circuit activity" is retained by only {{stab_C1_retained}} and **reversed** by all {{stab_C1_lost}} monotone specifications. Under the default rule imidacloprid at 1 µM drives `g_ach` to {{stab_default_g_ach}} and the network from {{stab_default_vehicle_hz}} to {{stab_default_treated_hz}} Hz; under a monotone rule the *same compound at the same engagement* takes `g_ach` to {{stab_monotone_g_ach}} and **excites** the network, {{stab_monotone_vehicle_hz}} to {{stab_monotone_treated_hz}} Hz ({{stab_monotone_pct_change}}). Suppression is a property of the −1.6θ² term, not of the integration.
* "The neighbourhood buffers nicotinic selectivity" is retained {{stab_C5_retained}} of {{stab_family_size}} and reversed by none, its {{stab_C5_undecidable}} failures being undecidable. The previous explanation — that the monotone rules "buffer at least as much, never less" — is **false**: over the {{stab_mean_gap_nicotinic_monotone_n}} reversing specifications with a defined index the mean gap runs {{stab_mean_gap_nicotinic_monotone_max}} to {{stab_mean_gap_nicotinic_monotone_min}} log₁₀ units, {{stab_monotone_buffer_less_n}} buffer *less* than the shipped rule ({{stab_monotone_buffer_less}}), and the aggregate is carried by the extreme.
* "The neighbourhood amplifies Nav/AChE selectivity" is the weakest amplification claim at {{stab_C6_retained}} of {{stab_family_size}}. Retained by every specification: {{stab_specification_independent}}.

The threshold grid tells the same story about conventions: the amplify/buffer split is **not** stable (`stable = {{thr_stable}}`), nicotinic buffering holding in {{thr_nicotinic_matching}} of {{thr_nicotinic_cells}} cells with a mean gap from {{thr_nicotinic_gap_max}} to {{thr_nicotinic_gap_min}} log₁₀ units and Nav/AChE amplification in {{thr_nav_ache_matching}} of {{thr_nav_ache_cells}}, flipping at the strictest corner ({{thr_nav_ache_failing}}, gap {{thr_nav_ache_gap_min}}), so we quote ranges and never the point estimate. T15 and T8 count different things — T15 the {{thr_n_compounds}} compounds of the two mechanism classes, T8 the whole library on both cuts — so {{thr_outside_t15_scope}} carries a circuit index in T8 and is outside T15's scope.

### 3.5 RQ3 continued: where the uncertainty lives, and what to measure next (Fig. F15, Tables T16, T17)

The Sobol' analysis ({{unc_evaluations}} model evaluations at n_base = {{unc_n_base}}, Var(Y) = {{unc_variance}} Hz²) resolves {{unc_n_resolved}} of {{unc_n_factors}} factors in the strict sense of a bootstrap first-order interval excluding zero: {{unc_resolved}} — the **engagement-to-gain transformation** (S1 {{unc_S_gain_transform}}, ST {{unc_ST_gain_transform}}) and the cut's **synapse-weight threshold** (S1 {{unc_S_weight_threshold}}, ST {{unc_ST_weight_threshold}}). Everything else is unresolved at this sample size ({{unc_at_noise_floor}}), including the external drive, whose S1 of {{unc_S_drive}} sits below the estimator's noise floor of {{unc_noise_floor}} on an interval of {{unc_CI_drive}}. The floor is set by {{unc_noise_floor_factor}}, not by the declared null factor `lif_seed` at {{unc_null_factor_S}}; the previous version of this section quoted the null factor and then ranked a factor inside the resulting noise. Interactions carry {{unc_interaction_share}} of the variance raw and {{unc_interaction_share_clipped}} with negatives clipped. On the analytic benchmark the estimator returns {{ish_estimated}} against the exact {{ish_analytic}} at n_base = {{ish_n_base}} (max error {{ish_max_abs_error}}).

The top factor is **{{voi_rank1_factor}}** ({{voi_rank1_var}} of {{voi_var_total}} Hz²), resolved by *{{voi_rank1_experiment}}* — reading the *shape* of the response-versus-engagement curve at a known synapse, which is precisely the assumption §3.4 shows the suppression conclusion turns on. The second, **{{voi_rank2_factor}}** ({{voi_rank2_var}} Hz²), needs **no experiment at all**: it is resolved by re-analysing synapse-confidence strata of data already held.

### 3.6 Supporting behaviour and literature concordance (Figs. F5, F7, F10; Tables T4, T5, T8)

Driving the {{veto_n_sweet_grn}} sweet GRN body IDs at {{veto_sugar_hz}} Hz and reading MN9 gives a complete bitter veto in vehicle on both engines ({{veto_rate_vehicle_ratio}} rate, {{veto_lif_vehicle_ratio}} LIF), partially relieved by fipronil ({{veto_rate_fipronil_ratio}}, {{veto_lif_fipronil_ratio}}), matching the published direction [7], [63]. The engines agree on **direction only** — sugar-driven MN9 is {{veto_rate_vehicle_sugar}} Hz against {{veto_lif_vehicle_sugar}} Hz, a difference following from the LIF background drive (§S3) — and we do not present that agreement as cross-model validation. The selectivity landscape splits by mechanism rather than potency, Nav/AChE compounds amplified ({{landscape_amplify_compounds}}) and nicotinic buffered ({{landscape_buffer_compounds}}), subject to §3.4's threshold caveat; compounds never reaching the circuit threshold on either cut ({{landscape_unscored_compounds}}) are reported unscored rather than buffered, and one of them is unscored for a substrate reason, chlordimeform's only insect mechanism acting through octopamine while neither cut contains an octopaminergic cell.

Literature concordance is reported per entry with its *n* (T4), never as a bare mean: of {{rank_evaluated}} evaluable entry–assay combinations, {{rank_n_degenerate}} order exactly two compounds, where ρ can only be ±1, and over the {{rank_n_informative}} with more than two the mean ρ is {{rank_mean_rho_informative}}. As a concordance count the result is {{rank_concordant}} concordant against {{rank_discordant}} discordant orderings (two-sided binomial *p* = {{rank_binomial_p}}); {{rank_skipped}} combinations are skipped for want of coverage and {{rank_known_discrepancies}} inversions are recorded rather than repaired ({{rank_discrepancy_ids}}). Of the {{rank_entries_evaluable}} distinct published orderings the library can cover, {{rank_shared_source}} {{rank_shared_source_verb}} from a publication the library already cites for a compound in that ordering and {{rank_shared_source_be}} therefore *not* out-of-sample, while {{rank_source_disjoint}} {{rank_source_disjoint_be}} source-disjoint; none is an independent test of the *circuit* model. The mixture arm is a **limited qualitative concordance check** ({{mix_n_matching}} of {{mix_n_targets}} verdicts reproduced, reference organism {{mix_species}}, {{mix_n_substituted}} partners same-class substitutes, §S4).

### 3.7 RQ4: reproduction (Fig. F1)

One command regenerates every figure, table and number in this paper from committed artifacts, with no download and no access to the 1.1 GB weight matrix:

```bash
python -m pip install -e ".[dev,viz]"
python scripts/reproduce_paper.py          # full run
python scripts/reproduce_paper.py --fast   # reduced permutation and replicate counts
python scripts/reproduce_paper.py --only dependence --outdir /tmp/x
```

Beyond the paper, `flylab run` turns a declarative spec into a self-describing run directory whose manifest records the spec hash, the code and library hashes, the seeds and two hashes per artifact, and the same spec and seed reproduce it byte for byte (`docs/WORKFLOW.md`). The paper's own output is 300-dpi figures with generated captions, the tables as CSV and Markdown, and `papers/results.json`, which carries every scalar quoted above with its value, rendered text, unit and producing step, plus a provenance block. `tests/test_reproduce.py` asserts that every key either document references exists in the record, that the record did not come from a `--fast` run, and — new in this revision, because the previous record's shuffle count did not match the shipped default and nothing caught it — that **every statistical parameter in the committed record equals the shipped default**.

The same wheel runs in a browser tab on Pyodide and is published as a static site, so these assays can be re-run by opening one link with nothing installed; `tests/test_browser_bridge.py` asserts **endpoint parity** — equal JSON, every readout, warning and provenance field, for a representative call to each route, which is route-level equality rather than a whole-analysis equality claim. MaleCNS v1.0 and FlyWire are CC-BY 4.0 from their consortia [5], [6] and must be cited before this software; licences are listed under Statements, and an archival Zenodo deposit will carry the DOI **[to be minted on acceptance]**.

## 4. Discussion

**What was learned computationally.** Three things, none of them a biological claim. *First*, typing the evidence changes results rather than documentation: refusing to model unsupported rows removed a class of small, plausible, invented responses no reader could have told apart from measurements, and grading a source's distance demoted a cross-species binding constant to a labelled proxy without changing its value.

*Second, and most important:* **the dependence method's own verdict is substrate-dependent**. The same instrument, validated against planted ground truth and with no parameter changed, classifies {{dep_land_composition_dominated}} of {{dep_land_cells}} cells as composition-dominated on one cut of MaleCNS v1.0 and {{dep_land_taste_composition_dominated}} of {{dep_land_taste_cells}} on another. The difference is structural and measurable in advance (T19): one is an in-star with mean degree {{named_mean_degree}} in which a degree-preserving rewire barely changes anything, the other has mean degree {{taste_motor_mean_degree}}. The generalisation we previously drew is withdrawn, and a requirement replaces it: a dependence verdict must be reported with the structural statistics of the graph it was measured on, and a negative should not be quoted until it has been repeated where the null has something to destroy.

*Third*, two instruments were themselves broken in ways that flattered the conclusions, and both were found by looking inward rather than outward: a transmitter null that moved the weighted excitation/inhibition balance while the supplement claimed it preserved that balance exactly, and a specification family whose alternative gain rules never reached the engine its topology predicates ran on. Two of those instruments, meanwhile, converge on one weak point: the direction of a nicotinic agonist's circuit effect is set by an asserted functional form, which is also the largest resolved factor in the variance decomposition and the top-ranked experiment.

**Model-generated observations versus encoded assumptions.** *Encoded*: the potency contrasts, each mechanism's direction, the biphasic shape of the nicotinic rule, the sign table of T21, the row normalisation of §2.2, and the transmitter predictions, themselves model output from another group [10], [12]. *Model-generated*: which effects survive which degradation and on which cut, the classification of the library, each ablation level's ordering information and its matched reference, the fraction of specifications retaining each conclusion, the variance attribution, and the ladder's own power surface. The separation is itself generated: an audit of the chain behind a circuit readout returns {{claims_chain_links}} links (T18), of which exactly {{claims_observed_phrase}} — the MaleCNS edges — is a measurement of the system being simulated, {{claims_literature_derived}} reuses a measurement of something else, {{claims_model_assumption}} are asserted modelling choices and {{claims_computed}} are arithmetic on the links above. A companion decomposition — a different decomposition, not a repartition of those nine links — reports {{claims_facts_phrase}}, {{claims_inference_phrase}} and {{claims_unknown_phrase}}.

**Relation to adjacent work.** Our version of the [69], [70] result survives neither a change of substrate (§3.2) nor a change of the engine's normalisation (§3.3), and both models normalise or regionally average, so the parallel is at least as consistent with a shared modelling artefact as with a property of receptor-weighted network perturbation.

**What the software cannot support.** It cannot predict a dose–response in an animal: there is no *Drosophila* pharmacokinetics to speak of, so a free concentration at the receptor is an *input*. It cannot support an insect-versus-vertebrate safety claim, nor a subunit-level selectivity claim for compounds whose sources used hybrid constructs — including the one *Drosophila* binding row, whose preparation resolves no subunit. It cannot support a general statement about whether receptor perturbation on connectomes needs topology, only a statement per cut with that cut's structure reported, nor absolute firing rates, nor anything about a living animal: there are no live data here and no code path can write any.

**Model-specific caveats**, discussed in full in §S9. (i) *The transmitter sign table is mostly asserted*: {{sign_asserted_transmitters}} carry no citation and are exempt from the specification family, and the weight-matched null bounds how much the *assignment* of transmitters to cells matters but not these numbers. (ii) *The row normalisation is load-bearing* (§2.2, §3.3). (iii) *Receptor effects are applied across transmitter-defined synapses*, which the expression layer relaxes as a bound rather than an improvement: coverage is {{expr_coverage_overall}} of cells, adult motor-neuron receptor expression is a confirmed gap, and weighting shifts MN9 by {{expr_mn9_delta_hz}} Hz at 1 µM imidacloprid. (iv) *Hops-limited cuts*: missing inhibition can flip the sign of any readout, and §3.2 quantifies how much that matters. (v) *The rate-versus-LIF discrepancy* (§3.6) is agreement of direction only.

**Prospective, not pre-registered.** The seven hypotheses in §S5 are *prospective*: written before any experiment, labelled `software_prediction`, `live_result` null. A mutable GitHub repository is not a registry, so they become pre-registered only once the tagged release is externally archived with a timestamp. Two of them, H2 and H3, predict a readout whose *sign* is a modelling choice over this paper's own specification family, and the supplement now says so beside them rather than only beside H6.

## 5. Conclusion

FlyLab couples typed pharmacological evidence to compound- and concentration-specific perturbation of named, synapse-resolution *Drosophila* circuits, and ships three tests the coupling makes possible: connectome-dependence analysis with a validated ladder and an explicit equivalence margin, an ablation ladder scored against a matched reference, and a conclusion-stability matrix, with a variance budget that turns into a ranked experiment list. Applied to its own results the method establishes that the most quotable circuit result is a property of an asserted functional form; that the ordering information lives in the mechanism rules and the transmitter composition, on a readout whose normalisation builds part of that in; and — the finding we would most want another group to try to break — that **the dependence verdict itself reverses between two cuts of the same connectome**, so a "this prediction does not need the wiring" result is only as good as the substrate it was measured on.

What would change the picture is short and specific, and the uncertainty budget rather than taste says what it is: a calibration of receptor engagement against synaptic gain at a known synapse; a reconstruction-confidence analysis, which needs no bench time at all; a repeat of the dependence landscape on a cut that is neither an in-star nor hops-limited; and one live *Drosophila* assay. Until then this is a description of a method and of a tool that tests itself.

## Statements

**AI assistance.** The author defined the research questions, methods and analyses; verified the source literature, checking each cited publication against the value attributed to it; executed and reviewed every analysis; and accepts full responsibility for the scientific content. AI assistance was used for software implementation and for drafting and editing prose. No data were generated, imputed, estimated or extrapolated by a language model; every number in the results, tables and figures was produced by the reproduction pipeline from committed artifacts and is keyed in `papers/results.json`.

**Animal research.** No animal experiments were performed and no live-animal data are reported. The `live_lab` field of every notebook produced by this pipeline is `null`. The protocols in `protocols/` are unexecuted drafts.

**Data and code availability.** Code: https://github.com/pinkysworld/FlyLab (MIT). Connectome data: official MaleCNS v1.0 and FlyWire releases, CC-BY 4.0, downloaded and not redistributed; derived cuts are committed under `data/derived/` with their citation strings. Literature datasets: `data/literature/`, each value carrying a DOI or PMID.

**Competing interests.** None declared. **Funding.** None.

